# CHRONOS — Commit & push hygiene

You **never** auto-commit or auto-push. The user is in charge of when work
ships. But long-lived uncommitted work in the tree is a hazard, so when the
user pivots to something new, you offer to push what's done first.

## When the user starts something new

A "topic switch" is when the user clearly moves on from the work just
completed: a new request unrelated to the prior thread, "let's do X", "moving
on", "now I want to...", etc. (Not: follow-up tweaks, bug fixes, or
clarifications on the same feature.)

At that moment, **before starting the new work**, check the tree state:

1. Run `git status` and `git diff --stat`.
2. If **all** of the following are true, ask the user if they want to push
   first:
   - **Substantial:** more than ~200 changed lines across tracked files, or
     a feature is functionally complete.
   - **Well tested:** the offline test suite passes (`pytest tests/ -m "not
     live" -q`); any new code added in this session has its own tests.
   - **In-session:** the changes were made this session, or the user has
     already acknowledged them.
3. If the user says yes, commit and push per the protocol below. If they say
   no, drop it and proceed with the new task.

If the diff is smaller than ~200 lines, do not interrupt — just start the
new work. The user can still ask for a push at any time.

## Hard "do not auto-stage" carveouts

Stop and warn instead of staging if `git status` includes any of:

- `.env`, `.env.*`, files matching `*.key`, `*.pem`, `*credential*`,
  `*secret*`, `*.token`
- Files larger than 1 MB
- Files outside the project tree
- Anything not explicitly listed in this session's diff scope when the user
  selected "this session only"

Surface these to the user. Don't bury them in a commit they didn't review.

## When the user explicitly asks to commit / push

Follow the existing system protocol (HEREDOC commit messages, no force
push, never amend pushed commits, never modify git config). The "ask before
new work" trigger does not apply when the user gave the command directly.

## After a push

Report compactly: branch + remote, commit hash(es), one-line title each.
Then move on to the new topic. Do not include the diff stat unless asked.

## Never

- Never push to `main` without explicit user confirmation, even on small
  diffs.
- Never include files the user hasn't seen ("scope_check: ask_first" is the
  default — when in doubt, list the files and ask).
- Never amend or force-push without an explicit user request.
- Never stage `.env` or secret-suffix files. If `.gitignore` is missing
  them, flag it and stop.
